  "slug": "machine-prediction-fde3",
  "summary": "Benchmarks and new models for CRISPRi, combinatorial and siRNA perturbation response now treat assay noise, label heterogeneity and graph bias as first-class terms.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is prediction of cellular and molecular responses to perturbation, with explicit statistical treatment of the noise and bias in the training labels. The Virtual Cell Challenge 2026 formalizes six metrics for zero-shot prediction of transcriptional responses to CRISPRi knockdowns, setting a shared evaluation target. GAIN predicts single-cell combinatorial perturbation responses by decomposing them into an additive baseline plus a learned non-additive interaction term. DeepCINET makes viability assay noise part of a ranking objective for cell line drug response, while siGMA uses a heteroscedastic multimodal formulation with calibrated uncertainty for chemically modified siRNA silencing efficacy across heterogeneous datasets. Related work corrects structural artifacts rather than label noise: Inverse Node Frequency normalizes metapath features to reduce hub bias in target-adverse event knowledge graphs. PTM-BDL and BrainRoute extend the theme to site-resolved phosphosite-aware drug response and community-verified BBB permeability.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
